WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.792
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.052 --> 00:00:09.010
BRENNAN DOYLE: Hello, my
name is Brennan Doyle.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.910
I'm a solution architect
at the Google Cloud,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:16.350
focusing mostly on media
and entertainment vertical.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.560
I have with me here
my friend Greg Denton,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.762
who's a software engineer
on our render team.

00:00:21.762 --> 00:00:23.970
We're going to talk to you
a little bit about OpenCue

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.622
and rendering.

00:00:25.622 --> 00:00:27.080
First thing we're
going to do is we

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.090
talk a little bit about what
problem are we trying to solve.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.280
And then I'll introduce
you to two different ways

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.650
that we can solve it on GCP.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.480
And then we'll do an
overview of a new tool

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.760
that we've open sourced
in collaboration

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.550
with Sony Pictures
Imageworks called OpenCue.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.300
And then we'll do a live
demo, fingers crossed.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.540
And then Q&amp;A at the end.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.050
So what problem are
we trying to solve?

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.720
So basically, there has
been growing pressure

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.980
of new content being created.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.690
So with Hulu, Netflix, all these
different content creators,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.140
there's been an explosion
in unique scripted content.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.462
So the need for creating
graphics has grown.

00:01:08.462 --> 00:01:10.920
In addition to that, if you
look at what we were doing back

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.750
in the 1990s, "Titanic," for
instance, had 300 shots in it.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.090
The last "Avengers" movie
had 3,000 shots in it.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.340
So each individual
project that comes out

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.910
is having like a 10x growth in
the amount of graphics that are

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.340
needed to finish that project.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.800
A somewhat popular
animation studio

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.650
claims that one of
their more recent films

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.590
took them about 33 hours
of frame to render.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.900
And when you look
at a two hour movie,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.840
you're looking at 24 frames a
second, 60 seconds a minute.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.120
120 minutes is about
172,800 frames.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.940
That's not only math, but
those are large numbers.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.090
When you start to multiply those
by how long it takes per frame,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:56.070
you're looking at 5,702,400
hours for a single revision

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.750
of an animated feature.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.280
And time equals money.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.670
So how do you go about
orchestrating these things

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.440
in a way that's efficient?

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.530
And that's what we're here
to talk to you about today.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.419
So at Google Cloud,
there are two ways

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.630
we can help solve
the rendering crisis.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.560
One of them is using
software as a service.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.150
So it's much easier
for you to set up.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.220
It's generally targeted
towards small and medium sized

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.150
businesses.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.130
And the other is building
a custom pipeline

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.053
using infrastructure
as a service.

00:02:25.053 --> 00:02:26.970
So the two things I'll
talk to you about today

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.650
are Zync Render,
which is a turnkey

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.120
solution for small and
medium business owners

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.270
to do rendering on the cloud,
mostly for burst rendering,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.880
and then also OpenCue,
which is basically

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.550
for larger studios that
have custom pipelines, how

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.130
do we add Google help you
to build something that'll

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.323
solve problems for you.

00:02:46.323 --> 00:02:47.490
So a little bit about Zync--

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.380
Zync is designed by
visual effects artist

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.850
for visual effects artists.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.220
It makes it extremely
simple to render.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.282
There are several components
that go into a Zync system.

00:02:57.282 --> 00:02:59.490
Basically, it integrates
with your existing rendering

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:00.100
software.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.640
So if you're using any
of these following tools,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.930
it will work with these.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.390
And it's very simple
to kick off a job

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.738
and render it in the farm.

00:03:08.738 --> 00:03:10.530
Basically, what you
end up with is a client

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.710
that manages all of our
transfer to the cloud

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.780
from your local workstations
to the cloud copying

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.090
all the data that is
required for your rendering.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.440
You install plugins inside of
your host 3D or 2D application.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.940
And then there is
a web console that

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.160
allows you to manage your jobs
after they've been submitted.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:28.800
So you can move
around priorities,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.010
do troubleshooting on jobs,
things of that nature.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.880
It's a very simple architecture.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.370
Zync would be for yourself.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.350
A lot of what we're
building out for you

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.860
is contained within
this Compute Engine node

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.660
right here, which
basically everything is

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.660
a black box behind the
scene happening for you.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.200
You don't have to worry
about that infrastructure.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.780
So basically, what
we're doing is

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.990
we're setting up
authorization for your users,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.090
and then managing all your
transfers and renders.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.640
So infrastructure
as a service is

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.670
more what we're going
to dig deeply into today

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.410
and take a look at OpenCue.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.720
But basically,
the larger studios

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.300
that we work with generally
have very unique pipelines

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.580
that require them to do
very specific things.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.730
So they need a tool
that is custom built.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:15.270
So in that world,
everything is a resource.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.089
Basically, each
piece of the puzzle

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.558
that is required to be
put together for rendering

00:04:20.558 --> 00:04:22.350
is a resource that you
need to orchestrate.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.190
So you start with
your render workers,

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:27.750
which are basically your VMs
in various sizes and flavors.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.940
And then you can add
your persistent disks

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.610
for storing data,
additional disks that

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.700
might be needed for
holding onto your pipeline

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.260
information, things
of that nature,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.260
object storage as a backup.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.873
And all these need
to be orchestrated.

00:04:41.873 --> 00:04:43.290
So when we stick
them together, we

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.400
end up with something
a little bit like this.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.360
So rendering VMs are the root
of what we're trying to achieve

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.220
is being able to render frames.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:51.840
We have some kind
of a storage, which

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.370
is where we are storing
all of our assets

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.550
that are going to be
prerequisites for renders.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.980
We generally have some
kind of NFS file server

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.530
that is required to
either host your textures

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.550
or your pipeline for
software and such.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.250
When we're dealing with
a hybrid conductivity,

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.038
we almost always are using
a read through cache.

00:05:11.038 --> 00:05:13.080
And then there's some kind
of license server that

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.430
needs to be created and some
way to manage your users'

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.050
permissions as well as
something for logging,

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.870
which we use Stackdriver for
that, and your asset management

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:24.710
database.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.830
And we're here to talk
to you about OpenCue.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:29.570
So for what we've
built here today,

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:33.080
we're using Terraform templates
to build out our infrastructure

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.910
and to manage
infrastructure as code.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.000
So it's easy to spin up,
modify, and move around

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.478
to different places
if we need it.

00:05:40.478 --> 00:05:42.520
So let's talk a little
bit about what is OpenCue.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.380
OpenCue is a render
orchestration layer,

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.570
or a queue management
system that we've

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.400
partnered with Sony
Pictures Imageworks

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.260
to release to the
open source community.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:53.120
So it's available for
you now on GitHub.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.640
I'll provide some links
here in just a bit.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.400
It has been used by Sony
on hundreds of films.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.760
It has been used in various
different deployments

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:05.420
as well on prem, hybrid,
and on the cloud only,

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.840
and has scaled to
many, many machines.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.010
So OpenCue can work in a
variety of architectures.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.010
This is not a tool that
we've released that only

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:14.630
works on Google Cloud.

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:19.640
It was developed in an
on-premise-- there we go--

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:20.870
way of working.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.730
And you can see how it
would function here.

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:25.970
It's basically used to
distribute the thousands

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:27.650
or hundreds of
thousands of frames

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.740
to all of the render hosts
that you are utilizing.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.890
It's also been used by
them quite consistently

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:35.180
for hybrid deployments.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:37.970
Here is an example of
a hybrid deployment.

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:41.630
Sony last year was discussing--
they are pretty consistently

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.570
spinning up like 60,000
CPUs in the cloud

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:48.365
and managing them with OpenCue.

00:06:48.365 --> 00:06:50.240
So the deployment we're
looking at here today

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.740
is basically what we are
calling a virtual studio.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.740
So at the moment, Greg's
going to do a demo here

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:55.340
in just a bit.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:59.090
He's running a virtual
workstation out of Oregon

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:02.870
that is connected to his
laptop running a software

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:08.400
client from Teradici and a
protocol called PC Over IP.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.300
So if you were setting
up a studio from scratch,

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.300
it's likely that you would
want to use something

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.090
like a zero client, which
is not a computer, but just

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:20.280
a device that allows you connect
to that PC Over IP stream

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.860
and to stream things
back and forth.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.517
You need some way to
connect to Google Cloud.

00:07:24.517 --> 00:07:26.600
In this instance, we're
using the public internet.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.100
If you were in
production, you would

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:31.107
be using either cloud VPN,
a dedicated interconnect,

00:07:31.107 --> 00:07:32.690
or you'd work with
one of our partners

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.840
to share a dedicated
interconnect line.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.850
So again, the
heart of the matter

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:39.830
here is a virtual
workstation running

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.210
on Google Cloud, which has an
Invidia T4 processor in it.

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:45.470
There we go.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:50.300
And it is how we're going to
drive our Myosin file demo.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.698
And behind that, we're only
using a single machine here.

00:07:52.698 --> 00:07:54.740
So we're not working
through a connection broker.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.600
But a connection
broker is a layer

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:58.640
that allows your artist--
if you have hundreds

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:00.710
of artists logging
in to a single point,

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.080
it directs them to their
particular machines

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.300
and also helps you to
manage their permissions

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.515
and log in data.

00:08:07.515 --> 00:08:09.140
So today, we're
running our storage off

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.660
of an Elastfile
scale-out file system,

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:13.830
which is able to tier
to cloud storage.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.890
So you have a
combination of ability

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:20.270
to customize it for high speed
and also use cloud storage

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.688
for low cost backup data.

00:08:22.688 --> 00:08:24.230
We also have a
license server running

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:27.930
in the cloud, a very small Linux
VM which is serving up our MIA

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:32.100
licenses and V-Ray, which we'll
be doing for rendering today.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.370
In addition to that, we
have our Cuebot server.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:37.700
So a Cuebot server
is a piece of OpenCue

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.630
that I mentioned earlier.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.559
It is managing the
distribution of the jobs.

00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:45.380
Maintaining the
state of our database

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:48.680
is an OpenCue database
running on Cloud SQL, which

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.440
uses Postgres SQL.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.400
And then we've got a
group of render workers

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:53.490
in the background.

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:56.300
So again, we've orchestrated
all of this using Terraform,

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.610
Packer, and Docker
and are currently

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.060
working on making those
scripts available to everyone

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.265
through open source.

00:09:03.265 --> 00:09:04.640
So the types of
things that-- the

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.640
costs that you will incur
in this kind of system

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:07.910
are all listed out here.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:08.910
I won't go through them.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:11.210
I'll let you take a picture
and think about them later.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:12.710
At the end of this
conversation, I'm

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:14.952
totally happy to answer any
questions that you have.

00:09:14.952 --> 00:09:16.910
We also have a booth on
the far side over there

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.430
if I want to come and
have a little deeper chat.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.830
We're way up against the back
wall there by the gaming team.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:27.830
So let's talk a little bit about
the architecture of OpenCue.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:29.900
So OpenCue has several pieces.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:31.640
We have the-- let me
see if this works.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:33.290
There we go-- the Cuebot server.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:35.450
The Cuebot server
is running right now

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:39.950
on a VM in Google
Cloud Platform.

00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:42.968
It is a Java service
that maintains

00:09:42.968 --> 00:09:44.510
the distribution of
the jobs and also

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.780
enables you to log in
with a GUI and to see

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.790
the state of your current Cue.

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.430
There is the Postgres
SQL database that's

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:53.600
running in the background.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.150
Those two are
communicating to maintain

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.510
a list of what jobs are
active and where all the log

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.670
files are residing.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:04.790
There is this RQD
Render host over here.

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:07.430
These are our managed
instance groups,

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:10.940
which are going to be
doing the rendering for us.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:13.340
They're running an RQD
process and communicating

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.215
over these ports back and
forth to the Cuebot server.

00:10:16.215 --> 00:10:17.840
In addition, on our
artist workstation,

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:19.130
we have a couple of things.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:24.290
A PyCue is a module that
enables us to communicate

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.810
between Cuebot and the
render hosts and the artist

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:27.800
workstation.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.750
We have a tool
called CueGUI, which

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:33.300
is the interface for seeing the
list of jobs that are running,

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.590
how we prioritize those,
and how we assign jobs

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.432
to particular render hosts.

00:10:38.432 --> 00:10:39.890
In addition to
that, we have a tool

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:42.140
called PyOutline,
which is assisting us

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:43.460
in submitting jobs.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:49.730
OpenCue ships with a
plugin for Maya and Nuke,

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:52.940
which enables you just from the
interface to pull down a menu

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:55.647
and say, Render to OpenCue.

00:10:55.647 --> 00:10:57.230
Also, there are
example scripts on how

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:59.520
you could build them for
your own customized pipeline.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.600
So since it's open source,
if you know some Python,

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.560
you can get in there and
script your own plugins.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:07.460
And then there's a
Submit Application,

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.050
which is running inside
of our host, too.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:12.530
So just talk a little bit
about the infrastructure

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:16.510
that we're seeing and how
we're orchestrating it--

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:18.460
to configure and build
our render hosts,

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:20.350
we're using Docker images.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:22.300
So basically,
those Docker images

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.380
are scripts that allow us to
define all the prerequisites,

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:28.570
libraries that need to be
installed as well as all

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:31.990
of the third party software,
and in some instances links

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:35.260
to where you can
check out licenses.

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:37.450
Those are stored in
our Container Registry.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:39.910
And when we launch
a machine, we're

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:41.860
launching an instance
template, which

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:45.850
is reading in that Docker file.

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:48.490
We also have the-- oh
jeez, went the wrong way.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:49.330
Pardon me.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:53.398
We also have the ability
to resize our render hosts.

00:11:53.398 --> 00:11:54.940
So at the moment,
what we're doing is

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:57.380
we're doing that manually
in this instance.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.320
But the logic is
there that, if you

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:01.990
wanted to integrate your own
instance or work manager that

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.670
would look at the
size of your queue

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:06.153
and automatically scale
your manage instance groups

00:12:06.153 --> 00:12:08.320
and look for health checks
and make sure that you've

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.440
got the machines you need, it
is quite straightforward to put

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.080
something together there.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.150
So I think that covers just
about an overview of OpenCue.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:19.720
Why don't we dive in and take
a look at the tool itself?

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:22.050
And I'll hand it over to Greg.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:23.900
GREG DENTON: All right.

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:24.570
Hi everyone.

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:25.512
Thanks for coming out.

00:12:25.512 --> 00:12:26.720
Yeah, my name is Greg Denton.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.680
I'm a software engineer
here at Google Cloud

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:32.750
in the Media and
Entertainment Rendering Team.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:37.340
And so if we switch
over to the demo box,

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:40.730
we'll jump right in and go
ahead and launch CueGUI.

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.420
So like Brennan
was talking about,

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:45.260
CueGUI is the
primary application

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:47.840
used for monitoring and
managing the render farm.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.750
It's a side PySide II
Python application.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:52.730
And it's going to
communicate back

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:58.095
with Cuebot, the central
server, with over GRPC.

00:12:58.095 --> 00:12:59.720
And if we look at it
right now, there's

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:02.820
absolutely nothing to look
at because we have no jobs.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:09.600
So we'll go over to a show and
go through launching some jobs.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.140
So there's two APIs.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:14.720
There's a general
purpose Python API

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.470
that's used primarily for
just any kind of interaction

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.930
with Cuebot.

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:24.590
Anything you can do in the UI,
you can do through the API.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:28.310
And then there's also the
PyOutline outline API.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:31.580
PyOutline is going
to help build jobs.

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.740
It gives you a lot
of functionality

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:37.070
to orchestrate multiple jobs
together and string them

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:39.150
together and submit them.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.510
So this is just a
simple Python script

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:42.980
that's using those two APIs.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:45.230
We'll go ahead and run it.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.690
It's kicked off three
jobs to the farm.

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:52.670
And you can see that they've
shown up over here in CueGUI.

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.620
And now that we have
some jobs, let's talk

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:59.520
about what a job actually is
as far as OpenCue is concerned.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:01.910
So a job is broken down
into three separate levels.

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:03.390
You have the job.

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:07.400
And that's really just a
container for the submission.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:11.510
And if we click on
one of them, we'll

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:17.060
see that it hosts information
about what user submitted it,

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:20.000
what facility it was submitted
from, what show it's for,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:22.430
what shot, that
kind of metadata.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:26.750
And then if you descend
down to a separate level,

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:29.390
the next one down
here is a layer.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:32.810
So a layer is going to be--

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.630
you can think of it as a
command to run with a frame

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:37.890
range associated with it.

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:40.400
You can have multiple
layers populating

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:44.640
a single job and dependencies
set up between those.

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:48.980
And then the layer
also is going to keep

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.570
track of the requirements
for that job.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:54.500
So if you want to say
that this type of command

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.280
is going to use four
cores in this example,

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:01.520
that's where you're going to set
that, same for memory and GPU

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:04.140
requirements.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:07.063
The next level down is
going to be the frame.

00:15:07.063 --> 00:15:09.230
And the frame is going to
be the individual instance

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:13.760
of that command with a
single frame, or frame chunk,

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:15.890
depending on how your
submission set up.

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:18.170
And it's going to be a
command that actually

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:21.090
gets run on the host itself.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:25.100
So we can see these guys are
all running and kicking away.

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:26.930
It's going to keep
track of things

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:29.750
like your runtime, the
exact amount of resources

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:30.920
that are used.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:33.080
And all those stats
are then bubbled up

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:35.040
to the higher levels.

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:39.740
So you get a summary view of
what has actually been running.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:45.350
So CueGUI is actually built up
as two separate applications.

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:47.780
You have Cuetopia, what
we're looking at here,

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:49.340
and CueCommander.

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:52.700
So Cuetopia is more
made for a single artist

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:56.150
looking at their jobs, trying
to see if their submissions are

00:15:56.150 --> 00:16:00.200
running, when they'll finish,
are they finished, any errors,

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:01.310
that kind of stuff.

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:05.720
CueCommander is more made for
a admin or render wrangler

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:09.110
to be able to get a higher
level view of the farm,

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.990
be able to have a lot more
controls over the management

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:12.600
of the farm.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:15.680
So as we dive a
little deeper, we'll

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:17.750
jump over to CueCommander.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:20.330
And the first thing that we're
going to take a look at here

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.560
is the Monitor Cue View.

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:26.930
So this is just a high level
view of everything that's

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:29.360
currently active in the farm.

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:34.260
And you'll be able to see
this across all users.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:41.180
And it gives admins the ability
to group jobs that are similar

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:43.110
and control them altogether.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.930
So for instance, if
you wanted to change

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:48.530
the priority of multiple
jobs altogether or change

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:53.230
the requirements, you
could do that from here.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:58.220
And so the next thing that
we're going to go over

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:04.220
is what groups
everything in OpenCue.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:08.896
And so the highest
level object is a show.

00:17:08.896 --> 00:17:13.250
And all jobs are going to
be submitted to a show.

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:15.710
You can see some really
simple information

00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:17.359
about what kind of
cores are running,

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:20.240
what frames we're
running for the show.

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:21.619
We only have a single show here.

00:17:21.619 --> 00:17:22.910
It's the testing show.

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:28.580
And we can do things from here.

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:31.070
I accept the maximum number
of cores, minimum number

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:33.140
of cores for jobs in that show.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:36.350
I'll enable and disable
booking, all that kind of stuff.

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:42.440
All right, so we've
gone over jobs a bit.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:48.900
Let's talk now about hosts.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:50.810
So if we open up the
Monitor Host View--

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.910
and we'll refresh
this populate it--

00:17:57.290 --> 00:18:00.090
this is going to show us
all of our render workers.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:02.780
So the render workers
right now are all

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.930
configured to be identified
by their IP address.

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:10.190
And you can also have
them configured to be

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:13.638
identified by their host name if
that makes more sense for you.

00:18:13.638 --> 00:18:15.680
It's pretty customizable
from that point of view.

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:24.830
And we can see that we have
3,000 render hosts all up

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:28.940
and running, thanks to
Adrian's demo from before.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:32.900
And they're all still kicking.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:37.100
So this view is
going to give you

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:41.360
a lot of information about
what type of host you have.

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.970
All of our hosts here are
running inside a single managed

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:44.960
instance group.

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:46.880
And like Brennan
was talking about,

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:49.770
that's running out of US West 1.

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:53.790
And they're reporting that
they have four cores--

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.580
all of them are idle right
now-- and 30 gigs of RAM.

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.730
So this is counting the
physical cores on the box.

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:08.040
So that correlates to an eight
core, 30 gig, an N1 preemptible

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:09.570
eight machine.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:15.870
So the other information that's
useful here is the ping time.

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:17.700
So it's showing you
when was the last time

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:19.710
that machine checked in.

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:21.870
If that goes beyond
a certain threshold,

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:26.160
Cuebot is going to consider
that machine down and stop

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:27.820
dispatching any additional work.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:31.620
And if there's any work that
was running on that that'll get

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:34.770
moved over to another host.

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:37.170
This works well for a
preemptible workflow.

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:39.210
Because our managed
instance group

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:43.590
is going to go ahead and
replace any instances that get

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:44.790
preempted.

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:49.260
And then Cuebot is going
to see that preemption

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:50.950
and reschedule the work for you.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:55.500
So it works entirely in
a preemptible fashion

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.870
if you want.

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:06.450
And so let's go ahead and
go back to Cuetopia here.

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:10.480
And we have a bunch of
our jobs have finished.

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:13.760
But we have a few that
are still running.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:25.200
And if we go to our hosts,
let's sort this by what's idle.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:28.140
So what I'm looking for
here is a host that's

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:29.983
actually running something.

00:20:29.983 --> 00:20:31.650
And one of the cool
things about OpenCue

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:36.210
is it has a way of
dynamically allocating slots

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:37.840
for jobs to run.

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:40.170
So like I was talking
about, all of these hosts

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:42.990
are four core hosts.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:47.010
And let's say if you
submitted a job that's

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:50.340
only going to require
one core, OpenCue

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.880
is going to carve out
what's called a proc.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.100
And that's what we're
showing down here.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.920
And it's only going
to carve out whatever

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:02.400
the job requires leaving
the rest of the procs

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:04.230
available to be used.

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:08.280
So for instance, you could
run four one core products

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:11.440
on a single host
or two two cores,

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:17.060
however your job in the
dispatcher decides to do it.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:20.870
So we have jobs and
hosts now covered.

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:24.760
How do jobs actually
get to hosts?

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:29.470
For that, we'll start talking
about how do we group hosts.

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:35.740
So there's a concept
called an allocation.

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:38.560
And an allocation is
really just a mechanism

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:41.930
for grouping multiple hosts
and controlling them together.

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:44.650
So we can see that all
of our render hosts

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:49.820
right now are running inside
this local general allocation.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:54.070
And that's going to be
identified by a facility where

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:57.190
the host is actually running,
and then a single tag

00:21:57.190 --> 00:22:04.270
that we want to pass to all of
the hosts in that allocation.

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:09.420
And then we have a subscription.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:12.470
And a subscription-- whoops--

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:16.120
a subscription is going to
be a way of providing rules

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:18.270
to an allocation.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:20.630
So if we select our
show, we can see

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:23.990
that the testing
show has subscribed

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:26.730
to the following allocations.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:31.200
And here, we can also specify
the amount of resources

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:34.350
from that allocation that
we want the host to have,

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:35.820
as well as a burst amount.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:38.400
The burst amount is going
to allow multiple shows

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:43.440
to share a single allocation
and be able to steal resources

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:44.370
when available there.

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:51.660
And so the next
thing-- if that's

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:56.250
our way of grouping
and managing hosts,

00:22:56.250 --> 00:23:01.090
services are our mechanism for
grouping and managing jobs.

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:04.450
So every job can be
submitted to a service.

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:06.930
In this case, we're looking
at the Arnold service.

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:09.630
And it's going to provide
some for default resource

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:11.940
requirements for that job.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:14.730
The job can specify anything
else it wants on top of that,

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:17.580
but these are going
to be the difference.

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:20.160
In this case, you're
able to specify that it's

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:21.120
going to be threadable.

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:24.360
It's going to be able to use
however many threads you want.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:27.600
And the minimum
number of threads

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:29.540
required-- this is
a percentage amount.

00:23:29.540 --> 00:23:32.760
So saying that it is
100 here means that it's

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:35.340
going to use one core.

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:37.870
You can obviously go above that.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:41.280
And then the maximum is
set at zero, essentially

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:44.250
saying that there's no maximum.

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:48.150
We are also specifying
minimum memory requirements

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:49.020
for that proc.

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:52.350
So it's only going to carve
out a proc from the host

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:55.810
if that memory is
available and if there's

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:57.760
any GPU requirements.

00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:01.060
The last thing that the
service is going to do

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:06.110
is apply these tags to
the jobs that are there.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:09.280
So now, putting
this all together,

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:15.750
if we go back to our Cuetopia
view and look at these jobs,

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:20.160
we can select the job and
look at its attributes.

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:22.740
And we can see that it
is, in fact, submitted

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:24.900
to the local facility.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:28.140
And it's using the testing show.

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:34.620
And then if we select our layer
and look at its attributes,

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:37.830
you'll be able to see that
it's using this shell service,

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:39.030
in this case.

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:42.900
And it's picking up the
util and general tags.

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:45.660
So with that information,
we're able to figure out

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:50.070
what allocation is
eligible to run that job

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:54.630
and what host it should go to.

00:24:54.630 --> 00:25:00.440
So if we go to our
job that's running

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:04.880
and select one of the
frames that has finished

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:07.710
or one of the frames
that's running,

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:11.930
we can view the
log for that job.

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:14.475
And we'll make this
a little bigger here.

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:21.070
And so this is just the
standard out and standard

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:22.900
error from whatever
process was running.

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:25.270
In this case, it's the VRA log.

00:25:25.270 --> 00:25:28.690
And if we look at
the succeeded one,

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:33.350
we can search here
for written image.

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:37.640
And that's going to give us
the path to the file that

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:39.410
was actually output.

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:44.300
We can go over here,
where I already have that

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:48.020
and launch RV.

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:51.770
And our frames are here.

00:25:51.770 --> 00:25:56.930
So that's launching and
running a simple job.

00:25:56.930 --> 00:26:01.200
This is just a single layer,
one command over multiple time

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:03.770
frames.

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:05.690
Things get a lot
more complicated

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:09.290
when you start talking about
dependencies and hooking up--

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:16.040
I want a layer to wait until all
the frames of one job are done,

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:18.920
or other connections.

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.530
OpenCue supports dependencies
at each of the different job

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:23.630
levels--

00:26:23.630 --> 00:26:26.250
job, layer, and frame.

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:29.250
And you can hook up as many of
those dependencies as you want.

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:31.040
So we jumped over
here to Maya now.

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:33.290
And I'll walk through what
it looks like submitting

00:26:33.290 --> 00:26:35.210
a job with dependencies.

00:26:35.210 --> 00:26:38.540
This is from our
robot dance party

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:41.240
animation that was produced
here at Next last year.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:47.540
And we've set it up this
time for V-Ray and Maya.

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:50.540
So it's all ready to go.

00:26:50.540 --> 00:26:54.360
And we'll launch the
CueSubmit plugin.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:56.190
Like Brennan was
talking about, this

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:58.560
is shipped as a
plugin inside Maya.

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:02.240
But you can also launch the
CueSubmit widget from a command

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:03.660
line if you want.

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:07.980
And you can configure it
to run any kind of command.

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:12.000
So we'll populate this
with some information.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:15.080
This is just basic job info.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:18.240
So the job name, all this
stuff is already in my history.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:21.800
So I'm just pressing
down to populate it.

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:26.480
And we'll do the lighting
pass and this is our Maya file

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:27.650
that we have open.

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:33.030
We'll select the render camera
and give it a frame spec.

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:38.880
So we'll just run
frames 230 through 250.

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:45.750
We'll set our Maya service and
give it a minimum core of four.

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:48.080
And for this layer, we
want it to run right away.

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:51.970
So we'll go ahead and leave
the dependency to none.

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:55.170
But we'll add another layer.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:28:01.560
And we'll say that this
is our FFmpeg pass.

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:04.110
So we want to do
something like run a make

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:06.120
movie after all the
frames are done,

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:10.500
so that way we can combine
everything together.

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:14.040
And I have a command
to do that over here.

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:24.370
We'll copy that in.

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:28.060
And since this is just going
to be a single command,

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:30.110
we only want it to run once.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:34.420
And we'll say it's just
going to run on frame one.

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:39.130
And for this, we'll leave
the default service.

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:42.320
And it's only going
to consume one core.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.400
And we'll set it to
be a layer dependency.

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:48.670
So that's meaning
that this layer

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:51.670
is going to wait until all the
frames from the lighting layer

00:28:51.670 --> 00:28:53.470
have finished.

00:28:53.470 --> 00:28:57.010
And we'll go ahead and submit.

00:28:57.010 --> 00:29:00.800
Get our confirmation,
close that.

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:07.590
And if we jump over to
Cuetopia, I'll refresh here.

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:10.120
And wait for our job.

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:11.400
There's our job.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:13.650
Now, it has come in.

00:29:13.650 --> 00:29:18.980
And if we double
click it, we can

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:21.770
see that there are
two layers here now.

00:29:21.770 --> 00:29:24.060
The lighting layer is
using the Maya service.

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:26.760
FFmpeg is using the default one.

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:29.270
And the frame range is
associated with that.

00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:32.390
And you'll see all the frames
for all the layers by default.

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:35.240
You can filter out
individual layers

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:38.730
if you want to view just
the frames from one layer,

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:41.160
but we'll leave it like
this for right now.

00:29:41.160 --> 00:29:44.240
And you can see that the
lighting layers are running.

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:47.750
And the FFmpeg layer
is marked as Depend.

00:29:47.750 --> 00:29:49.430
So it's going to
sit there waiting

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:52.670
until all of our running
frames finish up and go.

00:29:55.820 --> 00:30:00.422
And so a couple other things
I just wanted to go over--

00:30:00.422 --> 00:30:04.640
you saw me going up here to
this Views plugin menu a lot.

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:07.400
All these separate
widgets in CueGUI

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:10.220
are really built on
a plugin structure.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:12.650
So it's really easy with
just a little bit of Python

00:30:12.650 --> 00:30:16.160
to go in and add
your own widgets.

00:30:16.160 --> 00:30:19.160
If you want to
have a display that

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:22.790
hooks up to more historical
data, you can create that.

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:26.120
If you want something
that's tied into Shotgun,

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:27.830
you can build that out as well.

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:34.160
And that about covers
it for the demo.

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:40.160
We can switch over back
to the presentation.

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:44.110
And to give you
just an illustration

00:30:44.110 --> 00:30:46.840
of what was going on with
the dependencies, here's

00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:50.120
the dependency DAGs
that were submitted.

00:30:50.120 --> 00:30:55.160
So this was the lighting
render with the FFmpeg.

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:56.650
You can see that
the frames are all

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:59.710
feeding into the FFmpeg layer.

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:02.020
And that's how that
dependency is working.

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:04.360
But these kind of
dependency DAGs

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:06.580
can get pretty complicated
pretty quickly.

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:10.630
And OpenCue is going to
ensure that no frames are

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:14.260
run until after all the
dependencies are met.

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:18.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

